  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nBy Tuesday night, the cold front will stall out across northern\nMissouri and central Illinois and act as the focal point for \nrounds of showers and storm to redevelop late week. A nearly \nstationary longwave trough over the western CONUS and ridging \nover the Southeast will keep our upper-level flow out of the \nsouthwest through Thursday as weak shortwaves are pulled \nnortheastward into the Midwest. This will keep PoPs around \nTuesday night into Thursday as scattered showers and storms fire\nalong and north of the stationary front. With PWATS over 1.75 \ninches likely (70-90%) area-wide through Thursday, the flooding\nthreat will remain elevated, especially after the heavy \nrainfall tonight and Tuesday. There is a moderate chance \n(50-70%) of storm totals in the 2-3-inch range across eastern \nIowa through Thursday with localized areas of 4+ inches possible\n(5-15%). Rises on area creeks and streams are likely.\n\nHeading into Friday, a Front Range low in Colorado is forecast\nto form due to forcing from a lobe of mid-level vorticity pulled\nnorthward from the subtropics. This will introduce a more \nsoutherly component to the low-level flow as the stationary \nfront finally converts to a warm front and moves northward into \nIowa. The upper-level flow is expected to become zonal and force\nthe Front Range low eastward Friday night into Saturday. The \nincreased surface convergence will support another round of \nstorms before the same boundary is left behind for Sunday, \nkeeping PoPs around.",
